Your cluster eks currently has no node groups — it's running in Auto Mode. The guide below is a comprehensive production-readiness checklist for EKS with Managed Node Groups, structured so a new EKS engineer can follow it end to end.

EKS + Managed Node Groups — Production Readiness Guide
1. Cluster foundation
Kubernetes version
Always run a supported, non-EOL version. EKS supports the 4 most recent minor versions. Check current support:

aws eks describe-addon-versions --query 'addons[0].addonVersions[0].compatibilities[*].clusterVersion' \
  --output text

Run in CloudShell
Enable automatic minor version upgrades on node groups so nodes stay in sync with the control plane
Never let the node group Kubernetes version lag more than 1 minor version behind the control plane
Control plane logging
Enable all 5 log types — they are free to enable, you only pay for CloudWatch storage:

aws eks update-cluster-config --name <cluster> \
  --logging '{"clusterLogging":[{"types":["api","audit","authenticator","controllerManager","scheduler"],"enabled":true}]}'

Run in CloudShell
Private API endpoint
For production, disable the public endpoint and use only the private endpoint:

aws eks update-cluster-config --name <cluster> \
  --resources-vpc-config endpointPublicAccess=false,endpointPrivateAccess=true

Run in CloudShell
If you must keep the public endpoint, restrict it to your corporate CIDR:

  --resources-vpc-config endpointPublicAccess=true,publicAccessCidrs=["10.0.0.0/8"],endpointPrivateAccess=true

Run in CloudShell
2. IAM — the most critical layer
Cluster role (AmazonEKSClusterRole) — required policies
Policy	Purpose
AmazonEKSClusterPolicy	Core cluster management
AmazonEKSVPCResourceController	Security groups for pods (if used)
Node role (AmazonEKSNodeRole) — required policies
Policy	Purpose	Missing =
AmazonEKSWorkerNodePolicy	Node registration with cluster	Nodes never join
AmazonEKS_CNI_Policy	vpc-cni assigns pod IPs via ec2:AssignPrivateIpAddresses	All pods Pending
AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly	Pull images from ECR	ImagePullBackOff on all pods
AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore	SSM Session Manager access (no SSH needed)	Cannot shell into nodes
# Verify node role policies
aws iam list-attached-role-policies --role-name <AmazonEKSNodeRole>

Run in CloudShell
Use IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts) for pod permissions
Never attach broad permissions to the node role for application use. Use IRSA instead:

# Enable OIDC provider on the cluster
eksctl utils associate-iam-oidc-provider --cluster <cluster> --approve

# Create a service account with a scoped IAM role
eksctl create iamserviceaccount \
  --cluster <cluster> \
  --namespace <namespace> \
  --name <service-account-name> \
  --attach-policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/<PolicyName> \
  --approve

Run in CloudShell
3. Networking
VPC design
Minimum 3 subnets — one per AZ (us-east-1a, us-east-1b, us-east-1c)
Private subnets for nodes — nodes should never have public IPs
Public subnets for load balancers only — tag them correctly:
# Tag private subnets (for internal load balancers and nodes)
aws ec2 create-tags --resources <subnet-id> \
  --tags Key=kubernetes.io/role/internal-elb,Value=1 \
         Key=kubernetes.io/cluster/<cluster>,Value=shared

# Tag public subnets (for internet-facing load balancers)
aws ec2 create-tags --resources <subnet-id> \
  --tags Key=kubernetes.io/role/elb,Value=1 \
         Key=kubernetes.io/cluster/<cluster>,Value=shared

Run in CloudShell
IP address planning — critical for node groups
vpc-cni

pre-warms IPs on each node. A m5.xlarge can hold up to 58 IPs (15 per ENI × 4 ENIs). Plan your VPC CIDR to avoid exhaustion:

# Check current IP usage per node
kubectl describe node <node-name> | grep -A5 "Allocatable"

# Enable prefix delegation to increase pod density (recommended for large clusters)
kubectl set env daemonset aws-node -n kube-system ENABLE_PREFIX_DELEGATION=true
kubectl set env daemonset aws-node -n kube-system WARM_PREFIX_TARGET=1

Run in CloudShell
Security groups for pods
For fine-grained pod-level network isolation (e.g. RDS access only from specific pods):

# Enable on the cluster
aws eks update-cluster-config --name <cluster> \
  --resources-vpc-config securityGroupIds=[]

kubectl set env daemonset aws-node -n kube-system ENABLE_POD_ENI=true

Run in CloudShell
4. Node group configuration
Instance type selection
Use multiple instance types in one node group to reduce InsufficientCapacityError:
aws eks create-nodegroup --cluster-name <cluster> \
  --nodegroup-name prod-ng \
  --instance-types m5.xlarge m5a.xlarge m4.xlarge \
  --ami-type AL2_x86_64 \
  --capacity-type ON_DEMAND \
  --scaling-config minSize=3,maxSize=20,desiredSize=6 \
  --subnets <private-subnet-1> <private-subnet-2> <private-subnet-3>

Run in CloudShell
Spread across AZs
Always deploy node groups across at least 3 AZs. Use topology spread constraints on critical workloads:

topologySpreadConstraints:
  - maxSkew: 1
    topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
    whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule
    labelSelector:
      matchLabels:
        app: my-app

Managed node group update strategy
Set maxUnavailable to control rolling updates:

aws eks update-nodegroup-config --cluster-name <cluster> \
  --nodegroup-name prod-ng \
  --update-config maxUnavailablePercentage=33

Run in CloudShell
Use Bottlerocket AMI for security-hardened nodes
--ami-type BOTTLEROCKET_x86_64

Run in CloudShell
Bottlerocket is immutable, has a minimal attack surface, and supports automatic security updates.

5. Required addons — install and pin versions
Addon	Purpose	Install command
vpc-cni	Pod networking (IP assignment)	Pre-installed — keep updated
coredns	In-cluster DNS	Pre-installed — keep updated
kube-proxy	Service networking	Pre-installed — keep updated
aws-ebs-csi-driver	PersistentVolumes on EBS	aws eks create-addon --addon-name aws-ebs-csi-driver
aws-load-balancer-controller	ALB/NLB for Ingress/Service	Install via Helm
metrics-server	HPA / kubectl top	kubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server/releases/latest/download/components.yaml
# Check all addon versions and available updates
aws eks describe-addon-versions --kubernetes-version <version> \
  --query 'addons[*].{Name:addonName,Latest:addonVersions[0].addonVersion}'

# Update an addon to latest
aws eks update-addon --cluster-name <cluster> \
  --addon-name vpc-cni \
  --resolve-conflicts OVERWRITE

Run in CloudShell
6. Cluster autoscaler
Install Cluster Autoscaler to automatically scale node groups based on pending pods:

# Add required tags to node group ASG
aws autoscaling create-or-update-tags \
  --tags ResourceId=<asg-name>,ResourceType=auto-scaling-group,Key=k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled,Value=true,PropagateAtLaunch=true \
         ResourceId=<asg-name>,ResourceType=auto-scaling-group,Key=k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/<cluster>,Value=owned,PropagateAtLaunch=true

# Install via Helm
helm repo add autoscaler https://kubernetes.github.io/autoscaler
helm install cluster-autoscaler autoscaler/cluster-autoscaler \
  --namespace kube-system \
  --set autoDiscovery.clusterName=<cluster> \
  --set awsRegion=us-east-1 \
  --set rbac.serviceAccount.annotations."eks\.amazonaws\.com/role-arn"=<IRSA-role-arn>

Run in CloudShell
7. Observability
CloudWatch Container Insights
# Install CloudWatch agent + Fluent Bit for logs and metrics
ClusterName=<cluster>
RegionName=us-east-1
FluentBitHttpPort='2020'
FluentBitReadFromHead='Off'

curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/aws-samples/amazon-cloudwatch-container-insights/latest/k8s-deployment-manifest-templates/deployment-mode/daemonset/container-insights-monitoring/quickstart/cwagent-fluent-bit-quickstart.yaml \
  | sed "s/{{cluster_name}}/${ClusterName}/;s/{{region_name}}/${RegionName}/;s/{{http_server_toggle}}/On/;s/{{http_server_port}}/${FluentBitHttpPort}/;s/{{read_from_head}}/${FluentBitReadFromHead}/;s/{{read_from_tail}}/On/" \
  | kubectl apply -f -

Run in CloudShell
Key CloudWatch alarms to create
# Node CPU > 80% for 5 minutes
aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm \
  --alarm-name eks-node-cpu-high \
  --metric-name node_cpu_utilization \
  --namespace ContainerInsights \
  --dimensions Name=ClusterName,Value=<cluster> \
  --statistic Average --period 300 --threshold 80 \
  --comparison-operator GreaterThanThreshold --evaluation-periods 2

# Pending pods > 0 for 5 minutes (scheduling failure)
aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm \
  --alarm-name eks-pending-pods \
  --metric-name cluster_failed_node_count \
  --namespace ContainerInsights \
  --dimensions Name=ClusterName,Value=<cluster> \
  --statistic Average --period 300 --threshold 0 \
  --comparison-operator GreaterThanThreshold --evaluation-periods 1

Run in CloudShell
8. Security hardening
Pod Security Standards (replaces PodSecurityPolicy)
# Enforce restricted policy on production namespaces
kubectl label namespace production \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted

Run in CloudShell
Network policies — default deny all, then allow explicitly
# Default deny all ingress and egress in a namespace
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
  namespace: production
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  - Egress

Enable Secrets encryption at rest
aws eks associate-encryption-config --cluster-name <cluster> \
  --encryption-config '[{"resources":["secrets"],"provider":{"keyArn":"<kms-key-arn>"}}]'

Run in CloudShell
Enable GuardDuty EKS Protection
aws guardduty update-detector --detector-id <detector-id> \
  --features '[{"Name":"EKS_AUDIT_LOGS","Status":"ENABLED"},{"Name":"EKS_RUNTIME_MONITORING","Status":"ENABLED"}]'

Run in CloudShell
9. High availability for workloads
Pod Disruption Budgets — prevent outages during node upgrades
apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: my-app-pdb
spec:
  minAvailable: 2        # always keep at least 2 pods running
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app

Resource requests and limits — always set both
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "250m"
    memory: "256Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"

Without requests, the scheduler cannot make placement decisions. Without limits, a runaway pod can starve the node.

Liveness and readiness probes — always configure
livenessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /healthz
    port: 8080
  initialDelaySeconds: 30
  periodSeconds: 10
  failureThreshold: 3
readinessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /ready
    port: 8080
  initialDelaySeconds: 5
  periodSeconds: 5

10. Production readiness checklist — final verification
# 1. Cluster version is current and supported
aws eks describe-cluster --name <cluster> --query 'cluster.version'

# 2. All control plane logs enabled
aws eks describe-cluster --name <cluster> \
  --query 'cluster.logging.clusterLogging'

# 3. Private endpoint enabled
aws eks describe-cluster --name <cluster> \
  --query 'cluster.resourcesVpcConfig.{Public:endpointPublicAccess,Private:endpointPrivateAccess}'

# 4. Node role has all required policies
aws iam list-attached-role-policies --role-name <AmazonEKSNodeRole>

# 5. All addons up to date
aws eks list-addons --cluster-name <cluster>

# 6. No pods without resource requests
kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq '.items[] | select(.spec.containers[].resources.requests == null) | .metadata.name'

# 7. No pods without probes
kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq '.items[] | select(.spec.containers[].livenessProbe == null) | .metadata.name'

# 8. All nodes Ready across 3+ AZs
kubectl get nodes -L topology.kubernetes.io/zone

# 9. PodDisruptionBudgets exist for critical workloads
kubectl get pdb -A

# 10. Secrets encryption enabled
aws eks describe-cluster --name <cluster> --query 'cluster.encryptionConfig'

Run in CloudShell
The single most common production gap seen on node group clusters is missing AmazonEKS_CNI_Policy on the node role — the exact node-group equivalent of the AmazonEKSNetworkingPolicy issue you resolved today on Auto Mode. Always verify IAM policies first when pods are stuck Pending.
